{"entries":[{"caption":"10  Layers","key":"sec-day-2-layers","order":{"number":1,"section":[10,0,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"When creating a bar chart we first start with the raw data, then aggregate it to count the number of observations in each bar, and finally map those computed variables to plot aesthetics.","key":"fig-vis-stat-bar","order":{"number":2,"section":[10,5,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Steps for going from raw data to a table of frequencies to a bar plot where the heights of the bar represent the frequencies.","key":"fig-visualization-grammar","order":{"number":3,"section":[10,8,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"10.3 Geometric objects","key":"sec-geometric-objects","order":{"number":1,"section":[10,3,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"R has 26 built-in shapes that are identified by numbers. There are some seeming duplicates: for example, 0, 15, and 22 are all squares. The difference comes from the interaction of the color and fill aesthetics. The hollow shapes (0–14) have a border determined by color; the solid shapes (15–20) are filled with color; the filled shapes (21–25) have a border of color and are filled with fill. Shapes are arranged to keep similar shapes next to each other. ","key":"fig-shapes","order":{"number":1,"section":[10,2,0,0,0,0,0]}}],"headings":["introduction","prerequisites","aesthetic-mappings","exercises","sec-geometric-objects","exercises-1","facets","exercises-2","statistical-transformations","exercises-3","position-adjustments","exercises-4","coordinate-systems","exercises-5","the-layered-grammar-of-graphics","summary","sec-day-2-layers"],"options":{"chapter-id":"sec-day-2-layers","chapters":true}}